PART 2 -- A FALSIFIABLE PREDICTION, REGISTERED BEFORE ITS RUN
==============================================================================
PREDICTION (mine, written before the run below and reported either way):
  an offset-+1 condition-3 vertex needs the ENTIRE graph inside B_{rad+1}(w) with
  every centre on the sphere S_rad(w).  On a host whose a(.)-mean is high the ball
  B_{rad-1}(w) is large, so by (CS-1) the centre set is squeezed.  I therefore
  predict: at offset +1 the ratio |Ctr| / n is SMALL (below 0.35), and at higher
  offsets it is not systematically smaller.
  THIS CAN FAIL.  If offset-+1 vertices carry a large centre fraction, the whole
  mechanism story of round 39 sec.2 is wrong and I say so.

  offset   count   |Ctr|/n  min    mean     max
   +1        414         0.026    0.069   0.333
   +3        553         0.015    0.023   0.031
   +4         62         0.014    0.014   0.014
   +5         76         0.008    0.011   0.018
   +6        103         0.008    0.011   0.018
   +7         53         0.012    0.014   0.017
   +8         18         0.016    0.016   0.016
   +9         18         0.016    0.016   0.016

  PREDICTION HELD on its first half: every offset-+1 vertex has |Ctr|/n = 0.333 or less.
  SECOND HALF FAILED: offset +1's mean centre fraction 0.069 is NOT the
  smallest; some higher offset reaches 0.011.  The squeeze is not what
  separates offset +1 -- the separation must come from somewhere else.

==============================================================================
PART 3 -- (CS-1'), THE SPHERE BOUND REFINED BY THE ECCENTRICITY, PROVED AND CHECKED
==============================================================================
(CS-1) says a condition-3 vertex forces Ctr cap B_{rad-1}(w) = empty.  Under
ecc(w) = rad + t the complement of that ball is S_rad u ... u S_{rad+t}, and Ctr
lies in S_rad ALONE, so:

  (CS-1')  |Ctr| <= |S_rad(w)| = n - |B_{rad-1}(w)| - sum_{j=1..t} |S_{rad+j}(w)|.

  Proof: c in Ctr => d(c,w) = rad exactly, so c lies on the sphere S_rad(w) and on
  no other layer; the layers partition V.  QED -- strictly sharper than (CS-1),
  which discards the layers beyond rad.

  condition-3 vertices checked against (CS-1'): 1297
  instances where (CS-1') is TIGHT (|Ctr| = |S_rad(w)|): 300
  loosest instance: dense(s=7,n0=50)+C7 w=25, |Ctr|=1, |S_rad(w)|=32, slack 31

  WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT BUY, stated plainly: (CS-1') is a real sharpening and
  it is TIGHT on some instances, so it is not slack-for-free.  It still does NOT
  decide the open row, because bounding |Ctr| above never contradicts a SMALL centre
  set, and every offset-+1 instance found so far has a small one.  Naming that is
  the point: the open row will not fall to a counting bound on |Ctr|.

==============================================================================
PART 4 -- WHAT PART 1's TABLE ACTUALLY SAYS: A CONJECTURE, AND ITS LIVENESS
==============================================================================
PART 1's offset buckets are rad = 2 at offset +1, and rad = t at offset +t for every
t >= 3.  The second family is ecc(w) = 2*rad; the first is ecc(w) = rad+1 on hosts
whose DIAMETER is rad+1.  Both read as one statement:

  (RXM-PERI)  w maximally far from every centre  =>  ecc(w) = diam(G).

i.e. such a w is PERIPHERAL.  Tested on every condition-3 vertex of the family.

  condition-3 vertices tested : 1297
  (RXM-PERI) HOLDS at         : 1297
  VIOLATIONS                  : 0

  LIVENESS -- the test is a real restriction, not something every vertex satisfies:
    peripheral vertices among ALL 16247 vertices of these hosts: 9124 (56.2%)

  NO VIOLATION on this family.  A MEASUREMENT, NOT A THEOREM -- designed plus
  seeded-random, not exhaustive.  What it would buy IF proved, stated so the
  next round is not tempted to assume it: draft 42.4's open row needs
  ecc(w) = rad+1 AND condition 3, so under (RXM-PERI) it needs diam = rad+1.
  Route A2's hosts are a dense core with a long attached tail, where diam is far
  above rad+1 -- so (RXM-PERI) would make round 39 sec.2's emptiness a THEOREM
  on that class instead of a census.  It is not proved and is not used.

  PROVED, not conjectured (round 39): condition 3 => w is not a centre => ecc(w) >=
  rad+1.  And ecc(w) <= d(w,c) + ecc(c) = 2*rad for any centre c.  So a condition-3
  vertex always satisfies rad+1 <= ecc(w) <= 2*rad -- both bounds proved, and the
  table above sits strictly inside them.

